L000436	\noindent \emph{\textbf{Complete and Orthonormal Basis}.—}
L000437	In terms of the energy eigenbasis $|E_i\rangle$, fixed–$\mC$ states can be formally defined as
L000438	\begin{equation}
L000439	|\mC \rangle = e^{S_0} \int dE \, D(E) \phi_{E}(\mC) |E\rangle \,.
L000440	\end{equation}
L000441	All information about the state $|\mC\rangle$ is encoded in the wavefunction
L000442	\begin{equation}
L000443	\phi_{E}(\mC) \equiv \langle E | \mC \rangle \,.
L000444	\end{equation}
L000445	Using this representation, the matrix element entering the generating function of complexity can be defined as
L000446	\begin{equation}\label{eq:pole02}
L000447	\begin{split}
L000448	&\langle E_i | \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }}  | E_j \rangle  \equiv  \int d\mC  \, \phi_{E_i}(\mC)\phi^\ast_{E_j}(\mC)  \, e^{-\alpha \mC } \,.  \\
L000449	\end{split}
L000450	\end{equation}
L000451	As shown in the main text, the characteristic pole structure in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pole} is both necessary and sufficient for linear growth of holographic complexity in the semiclassical regime. This particular pole structure is realized by an infinite class of operators, including the geodesic length operator in JT gravity~\cite{Miyaji:2024ity} and the chord-number operator in the double-scaled SYK model~\cite{Miyaji:2025ucp}. The latter is also equivalent to the Krylov spread complexity operator. From the perspective of the wavefunction $\phi_E(\mC)$, the origin of this pole can be traced to its characteristic oscillatory behavior. In particular, for $E_i\simeq E_j$ one finds
L000452	\begin{equation}\label{eq:wavefunction}
L000453	    \phi_{E_i}(\mC)\phi^\ast_{E_j}(\mC) \underset{E_i\approx E_j}{\approx} \frac{1}{e^{S_0} \pi \bar{D}(\bar{E}) M} \cos \left( t_\mC E_{ij} \right) \,,
L000454	\end{equation}
L000455	with $t_\mC \approx \mC/M$. Substituting this form into Eq.~\eqref{eq:pole02} immediately reproduces the pole structure in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pole}.
L000456	
L000457	
L000458	An alternative approach to computing the expectation value of complexity, introduced in \cite{geodesic}, is based on the squared overlap between a fixed–$\mC$ state and the thermofield double state,
L000459	\begin{equation}
L000460	\begin{split}
L000461	     &P_t(\mC):= \langle  \text{TFD} (t) | \mC \rangle  \langle\mC|\text{TFD}(t)\rangle   \\
L000462	     &= \frac{e^{2S_0}}{Z} \int dE_i dE_j \, e^{-iE_{ij}t} \, \phi_{E_i}(\mC)\phi^\ast_{E_j}(\mC) \, \overline{\langle  D(E_i) D(E_j) \rangle}  \,.
L000463	     \end{split}
L000464	\end{equation}
L000465	Using the characteristic wavefunction \eqref{eq:wavefunction}, one finds that the dominant contribution depends only on $|t-t_\mC|$, since
L000466	\begin{equation}
L000467	e^{-iE_{ij}t} \, \phi_{E_i}(\mC)\phi^\ast_{E_j}(\mC)  \sim e^{\pm i E_{ij} (t-t_\mC)} \,.
L000468	\end{equation}
L000469	Crucially, we highlight that this squared overlap incorporates non-perturbative corrections because it is involved with the ensemble-averaged spectral correlator, \ie
L000470	\begin{equation}
L000471	  e^{2S_0} \,\overline{ \langle D(E_i)D(E_j)\rangle }=  R_2(E_i, E_j) +  \delta(E_{ij})  e^{S_0}D(E_i) \,.
L000472	\end{equation}
L000473	Equipped with this ``probability distribution", the expectation value of generating function and complexity measures can then be formally obtained as
L000474	\begin{equation}\label{eq:genC02}
L000475	\langle e^{-\alpha \mC } \rangle  = \int  d \mC \, P_t(\mC) \times  e^{-\alpha \mC} \,  \,,
L000476	\end{equation}
L000477	and
L000478	\begin{equation}\label{eq:defineC02}
L000479	\langle \hat{\mC} \rangle  = \int  d \mC \, P_t(\mC) \times  \mC \,  \,,
L000480	\end{equation}
L000481	respectively. However, this integral (\ie sum over all basis states) generically results in the divergence of $\langle \hat{\mC} \rangle $ due to contributions from $t_\mC\gg T_{\mt{H}}$. In particular, $P_t(\mC)$ develops a plateau for $t_\mC\gg T_{\mt{H}}$, with $P_t(\mC)\simeq \frac{2}{M T_{\mt{H}}}$. This divergence reflects the over-completeness of the original $\mC$ basis, which in turn originates from spacetime wormhole contributions.
L000482	
L000483	
L000484	In order to not only remove the divergence but also obtain a physically meaningful operator, one must perform a Gram–Schmidt orthogonalization~\cite{Miyaji:2024ity,Miyaji:2025ucp}, yielding an orthonormal basis $|\mC_O\rangle$. The choice of orthogonalization is directly tied to the physical interpretation of the resulting operator $\hat{\mC}_O$ in the classical regime, in close analogy with non-isometric codes~\cite{Akers:2021fut,Akers:2022qdl,Antonini:2024yif}. The operator $\hat{\mC}_O$ is defined in terms of Eq.~\eqref{eq:defineCO}, and its expectation value reads
L000485	\begin{equation}
L000486	\langle \hat{\mC}_O \rangle  = \sum_{\text{orthonormal basis}}  P_t(\mC_O) \times  \mC_O \,  \,,
L000487	\end{equation}
L000488	with $\sum_{\mC_O}  P_t(\mC_O) =1$. In the following, we still denote the total dimension of orthonormal $\mC_O$-basis as the partition function $Z$. In the following, we aim to show that the same universal time evolution can be derived by evaluating $\langle \hat{\mC}_O \rangle$, \ie presenting a long-time linear growth and a transition to the plateau at late times.
L000489	
L000490	
L000491	
L000492	In the semiclassical regime $t\ll T_{\mathrm H}$, only the disconnected part of the spectral correlator contributes. Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:wavefunction}, we can easily find that the squared overlap is dominated by
L000493	\begin{equation}
L000494	\begin{split}
L000495	     P_t(\mC) &\approx
L000496	     \frac{2}{\pi M\Delta E}
L000497	     \frac{\sin^2\left((t_{\mC}-t)\frac{\Delta E}{2}\right)}{\big(t_{\mC}-t\big)^2} \,,
L000498	     \end{split}
L000499	\end{equation}
L000500	This expression reveals that the TFD state is sharply localized around $t_\mC \approx t$.
L000501	Corrections due to orthogonalization are suppressed by powers of $e^{-S_0}$, so both the wavefunction structure~\eqref{eq:wavefunction} and the pole structure~\eqref{eq:pole} remain valid to leading order. As a result, the expectation value $\langle \hat{\mC}_O \rangle$ reproduces the expected linear growth at early times, namely
L000502	\begin{equation}
L000503	\langle \hat{\mC}_{O}  \rangle \big|_{t \ll T_{\mt{H}}} \approx  M \,t \,.
L000504	\end{equation}
L000505	
L000506	
L000507	As the evolution approaches the Heisenberg time $t \sim T{\mt{H}}$, the overlap becomes dominated by states with $\mC_O < Mt$, causing the linear growth to slow down. In the late-time regime $t\gg T_{\mathrm H}$, the probability distribution takes the form
L000508	\begin{equation}
L000509	   P_t(\mC_O) \approx\frac{1}{Z} +\frac{1}{Z}\int dE_idE_j \phi_{E_i}\phi^\ast_{E_j}R_2(E_i,E_j)e^{iE_{ij}t}     \,.
L000510	\end{equation}
L000511	The crucial point is that off-diagonal contributions are suppressed at late times due to level repulsion, $R_2(E_i,E_j)\sim \mathcal{O}(|E_{ij}|^\beta)$, with $\beta=2$ for GUE. As a consequence of this suppression, the probability distribution at late times becomes approximately uniform,
L000512	\begin{equation}
L000513	P_t(\mC_O) \approx \frac{1}{Z} + \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{t}\right) \,.
L000514	\end{equation}
L000515	Here, the constant is fixed by the completeness of orthonormal $\mC_O$-basis. Accordingly, the late-time expectation value saturates to a constant, \ie
L000516	\begin{equation}
L000517	\langle \hat{\mC}_{O}  \rangle \big|_{t \gg T_{\mt{H}}} \approx \frac{1}{Z}\sum_{\mC_O-\text{basis}} \mC_O \,.
L000518	\end{equation}
L000519	Finally, we recover the same universal time evolution, characterized by linear growth and late-time saturation, in agreement with the result obtained from the regularization of generating functions in the $\alpha \to 0$ limit.
