L000744	\subsection{A.2 Regularization and $\alpha
L000745	\to 0$ limit}
L000746	
L000747	After computing the generating functions of complexity, one can extract the time evolution of holographic complexity measures by taking the limit $\alpha \to 0 $. However, this procedure is potentially divergent due to the the continuum approximation of the spectrum, as well as the over-completeness of the infinite $\mathcal{C}$-basis. To regulate these divergences and obtain physically meaningful results with respect to a complete and discrete basis, it is essential to carefully treat the terms involving products of Dirac delta functions, \ie those arising from the sum over diagonal contributions in the spectrum. Accordingly, one may define a regularized operator by explicitly subtracting the divergent component from $\widehat{e^{-\alpha \mathcal{C}}}$, thereby yielding a finite and well-defined result.
L000748	
L000749	
L000750	A simple illustrative example arises from considering the identity operator, defined as
L000751	\begin{equation}
L000752	 \hat{\mathbbm{1}} : = e^{S_0} \int dE \, D(E) \, |E\rangle \langle E|  \,.
L000753	\end{equation}
L000754	By definition, the \textit{ensemble-averaged} expectation value of the identity operator remains unchanged, namely:
L000755	\begin{equation}\label{eq:averageone}
L000756	 \langle \text{TFD} |  \hat{\mathbbm{1}} |\text{TFD} \rangle  =\langle \text{TFD} | \text{TFD} \rangle  =  1  \,.
L000757	\end{equation}
L000758	However, let us now attempt to evaluate this average by explicitly expanding the spectral two-point correlation function:
L000759	\begin{equation}
L000760	\begin{split}
L000761	 \langle \text{TFD} | \hat{\mathbbm{1}} |\text{TFD} \rangle  &=  \frac{e^{2S_0}}{Z} \int dE_idE_j \, e^{-iE_{ij}t} \langle  D(E_i) D(E_j) \rangle  \times  \langle E_i | \hat{\mathbbm{1}}  | E_j \rangle  \\
L000762	&= \frac{e^{2S_0}}{Z} \int dE_idE_j \, e^{-iE_{ij}t} \langle  D(E_i) D(E_j) \rangle  \times  \frac{\delta(E_i-E_j)}{e^{S_0} D(E_i)  }  \,.
L000763	\end{split}
L000764	\end{equation}
L000765	This explicit decomposition introduces a problem because the exact (ensemble-averaged) decomposition of the spectral two-point function already contains a Dirac delta function,
L000766	\begin{equation}
L000767	   \overline{ \langle D(E_i)D(E_j)\rangle }= e^{-2S_0} \, \overline{\langle \rho(E_i)\rho(E_j)\rangle } =  e^{-2S_0}\left( R_2(E_i, E_j) +  \delta(E_i-E_j) \rho(E_i) \right)\,.
L000768	\end{equation}
L000769	Thus, when this expression is multiplied by another Dirac delta function $\delta(E_i - E_j)$ from the matrix element $\langle E_i | \hat{\mathbbm{1}} | E_j \rangle$, the integrand includes a product of two delta functions $\delta(E_i - E_j)^2$, which is ill-defined as a distribution.
L000770	
L000771	However, it is important to emphasize that this type of superficial divergence is merely an artifact of working in the continuum limit for simplicity. In reality, the Hilbert space under consideration has a finite dimension when $S_0$ is finite. Specifically, the total number of microcanonical states is given by
L000772	\begin{equation}
L000773	Z \equiv e^{S_0} \int_{E_0-\Delta E / 2}^{E_0+\Delta E / 2} D(E) d E \approx e^{S_0}  \bar{D}\left(E_0\right) \Delta E  \,,
L000774	\end{equation}
L000775	which is manifestly finite for any finite $S_0$. Assuming a finite and discrete spectrum,
L000776	\begin{equation}
L000777	 E_i \in \{ E_1, E_2, \cdots E_N\} \,,
L000778	\end{equation}
L000779	the total number of states is given by
L000780	\begin{equation}
L000781	N =  \sum_{i}^{N} 1 \quad \longleftrightarrow \quad Z : = \int d E \,   e^{S_0} D(E) \approx e^{S_0}  \bar{D}(E) \Delta E  \,.
L000782	\end{equation}
L000783	The continuum limit is formally obtained by replacing the discrete sum with
L000784	\begin{equation}
L000785	   \sum_{i}^N   \quad \longleftrightarrow \quad \int_{\Delta E} \rho(E) d E = \int_{\Delta E} dE  \,e^{S_0} D(E) \,.
L000786	\end{equation}
L000787	Additionally, the normalization of energy eigenstates determines the correspondence between the Kronecker delta and the Dirac delta function:
L000788	\begin{equation}\label{eq:doubledeltaij}
L000789	 \delta_{ij}  \longleftrightarrow \langle E_i | E_j \rangle = \frac{\delta\left(E_i-E_j\right)}{e^{S_0}  \bar{D}(E_i)}   \,.
L000790	\end{equation}
L000791	From the perspective of the discrete spectrum, it is clear that multiple summations over diagonal spectrum remain finite. For instance,
L000792	\begin{equation}
L000793	\sum_{i}^N \delta_{ij} \sum_{j}^N \delta_{ij} \times f(E_i, E_j)  = \left(\sum_{i}^N \delta_{ij} \times f(E_i, E_j) \right) \times N  \,,
L000794	\end{equation}
L000795	is well-defined. Translating this to continuous integrals, terms involving products of delta functions must be regularized accordingly. In particular, the extra delta function in $\delta(E_i - E_j)^2$ should be interpreted as the inverse mean level spacing:
L000796	\begin{equation}
L000797	\delta(E_i - E_j) \sim \text { mean level spacing } \sim \frac{\Delta E}{N} \approx e^{S_0} D(E_i) \,,
L000798	\end{equation}
L000799	More explicitly, the product of two delta functions is regularized in terms of
L000800	\begin{tcolorbox}[title={Regularization of multiple product of Dirac delta functions},colbacktitle=red!10!white, colback=blue!15!white,coltitle=red!70!black,top=-4pt, bottom=2pt]
L000801	    \begin{equation}\label{eq:twodelta}
L000802	    \begin{split}
L000803	    \delta(E_i - E_j) \delta(E_i - E_j)     &\Longrightarrow  \delta(E_i - E_j) \times e^{S_0} D(E_i)
L000804	    \end{split}
L000805	    \end{equation}
L000806	\end{tcolorbox}
L000807	This regularization prescription can be made precise using a smooth test function $f(E_i, E_j)$:
L000808	\begin{equation}
L000809	\begin{split}
L000810	&\int dE_i dE_j  \, f(E_i, E_j) \times \delta(E_i - E_j)\times \delta(E_i - E_j)  \\
L000811	&\Rightarrow \int dE_i dE_j  \, f(E_i, E_j) \delta(E_i - E_j) \times e^{S_0} D(E_i) \\
L000812	&= \int dE_i \, f(E_i, E_i) \times  e^{S_0} D(E_i) \,.
L000813	\end{split}
L000814	\end{equation}
L000815	which is the continuous counterpart of the discrete relation in Eq.~\eqref{eq:doubledeltaij}.
L000816	
L000817	As an exercise and consistency check for the regularization scheme, let us revisit the expectation value of the identity operator from Eq.~\eqref{eq:averageone}. First, using the level repulsion condition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:levelGUE},
L000818	\begin{equation}
L000819	R_2 (E_i, E_j) \times \delta (E_i- E_j) =0  \,,
L000820	\end{equation}
L000821	we find that
L000822	\begin{equation}
L000823	\begin{split}
L000824	  \langle D(E_i)D(E_j)\rangle \times \delta(E_i - E_j) &= \left( e^{-2S_0} R_2^{(\rho)}(E_i, E_i) +    e^{-S_0} D(E_i) \delta(E_i -E_j) \right)   \delta(E_i -E_j)  \\
L000825	  &= e^{-S_0} D(E_i) \delta(E_i -E_j) \delta(E_i -E_j) \,.
L000826	 \end{split}
L000827	 \end{equation}
L000828	Applying the regularization rule from Eq.~\eqref{eq:twodelta}, we obtain the physically consistent result, \ie
L000829	\begin{equation}
L000830	 \boxed{  \langle D(E_i)D(E_j)\rangle \times \delta(E_i - E_j)  \Longrightarrow  D(E_i) D(E_i) \delta(E_i -E_j) \,. }
L000831	\end{equation}
L000832	This leads directly to the expected normalization:
L000833	\begin{equation}\label{eq:doubledelta}
L000834	\begin{split}
L000835	\langle \text{TFD} | \hat{\mathbbm{1}} |\text{TFD} \rangle   &=  \frac{e^{2S_0}}{Z} \int dE_i dE_j  \, \left( D(E_i) D(E_i) \delta(E_i -E_j) \right)\times \frac{1}{e^{S_0} D(E_i)} \\
L000836	&= \frac{1}{Z} \int dE_i \,  e^{S_0}D(E_i) \\
L000837	&=1 \,.
L000838	\end{split}
L000839	\end{equation}
L000840	Next we consider $\alpha\rightarrow 0$ limit of the generating function for a complete basis. In this limit, the matrix element becomes
L000841	\begin{equation}\label{eq:completematrixelement}
L000842	\lim_{\alpha \to 0}  \langle E_i | \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }}| E_j \rangle   \rightarrow \langle E_i | E_j \rangle  = \frac{\delta(E_i -E_j)}{e^{S_0}\bar{D}(\bar{E})} \,.
L000843	\end{equation}
L000844	Since the average level spacing is given by $T_{\mt{H}}^{-1}$, the discreteness of the spectrum becomes significant in this limit. It is therefore necessary to properly regularize the contribution arising from summing over all diagonal terms, \ie $G_{\rm delta}$ term:
L000845	\begin{equation}
L000846	\begin{split}
L000847	\lim_{\alpha \to 0}  \underbrace{G_{\rm delta}(\alpha)}_{\text{It is a constant}} &\approx \int  \, d E_{ij} \, e^{-i E_{ij}t}  \delta(E_i - E_j) \times  \underbrace{\left[ \frac{\delta(E_i -E_j)}{e^{S_0}D(\bar{E})}  \right]}_{\text{need to be normalized} \to 1 }  \longrightarrow 1 \,. \\
L000848	\end{split}
L000849	\end{equation}
L000850	The regularization of multiple product of Dirac delta functions introduced in Eq.~\eqref{eq:twodelta} indicates that the $\alpha \to 0$ limit of the contact term should be regularized by
L000851	\begin{tcolorbox}[colbacktitle=red!10!white, colback=blue!15!white,coltitle=red!70!black,top=-4pt, bottom=2pt]
L000852	\begin{equation}\label{eq:regdelta}
L000853	 \lim_{\alpha \to 0}  G_{\rm delta}^{\rm (reg)}(\alpha)  = 1 \,, \qquad (\text{complete basis})  \,.
L000854	\end{equation}
L000855	\end{tcolorbox}
L000856	As a result, the physically well-defined $\alpha \to 0$ limit of the generating function of complexity is given by
L000857	\begin{equation}\label{eq:alphapluszero}
L000858	 \boxed{\lim_{\alpha \to 0}\langle \text{TFD} | \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }} | \text{TFD} \rangle  \equiv   \lim_{\alpha \to 0} \langle \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }} \rangle = \lim_{\alpha \to 0} G_{\rm delta}^{\rm (reg)}(\alpha)  = 1 \,.}
L000859	\end{equation}
L000860	
L000861	
L000862	Interestingly, all divergences arising in the limit
L000863	$\alpha \to 0$ can be attributed to a single divergent term proportional to $1/\alpha$ because $\alpha$ parameter in the generating function $\langle \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }}\rangle$ plays the role of a regulator for the over-complete basis. This observation motivates the definition of a regularized operator designed to subtract unphysical divergences. It is straightforward to notice that the regularized operator should take the form
L000864	\begin{equation}
L000865	  \boxed{  \widehat{\left(e^{-\alpha \mathcal{C}}\right)}_{\mathrm{reg}} \approx \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mathcal{C}}}-\hat{\mathbb{I}}\left(\frac{2}{\tilde{\alpha} T_{\mt{H}}}-1+\frac{\talpha}{3} T_{\mt{H}}+\mathcal{O}\left(\alpha^2\right)\right) }\,.
L000866	\end{equation}
L000867	This prescription precisely reproduces the result obtained from the regularized diagonal contribution $ G_{\rm delta}^{\rm (reg)}(\alpha) $, namely
L000868	\begin{equation}
L000869	 \lim_{\alpha \to 0} \langle (\widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }})_{\text{reg}} \rangle =1= \lim_{\alpha \to 0} \langle \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }} \rangle_{\text{reg}} := \lim_{\alpha \to 0} \left(  G_{\rm classical}(\alpha, t) + G^{\text{(reg)}}_{\rm delta}(\alpha) +G_{\rm sine}(\alpha, t)\right) \,.
L000870	\end{equation}
L000871	The determination of subleading terms in this expansion will be addressed in the following subsection.
L000872	
L000873	\subsection{A.3 Time evolution of holographic complexity measures}
L000874	From the generating function of complexity, one can compute the expectation value of the complexity operator via
L000875	\begin{equation}\label{eq:defineC}
L000876	\boxed{ \langle \hat{C} \rangle \equiv \langle  \text{TFD}(t)|\, \hat{C} \, |\text{TFD}(t) \rangle = \lim_{\alpha \to 0} \left(  - \partial_\alpha   \langle \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mathcal{C}}} \rangle  \right)_{\mathrm{reg}}  \,. }
L000877	\end{equation}
L000878	See, for example, \cite{Yang:2018gdb,Iliesiu:2021ari,Heller:2024ldz,geodesic} for previous studies of geodesic length in JT gravity. However, we emphasize that this expression,  as well as the operator $\hat{C}$, is in general ill-defined if one naively uses an over-complete $\mC$-basis. In this work, we assume the existence of a complete basis ${|\mC\rangle}$.
L000879	
L000880	By carefully taking the $\alpha \to 0$ limit while avoiding divergences due to the continuum approximation, we may extract the time dependence of complexity either by taking the derivative as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:defineC}, or via a series expansion:
L000881	\begin{equation}
L000882	\boxed{\langle (\widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }})_{\text{reg}} \rangle  \approx  1 - \alpha  \,  \langle \hat{C} \rangle   + \mathcal{O}(\alpha^2) \,.}
L000883	\end{equation}
L000884	Using the regularized contact term, we define its series expansion with $\alpha \sim 0$ as
L000885	\begin{equation}
L000886	G^{\rm (reg)}_{\rm delta}(\alpha) \approx  1 + \alpha \, \text{Constant} +  \mathcal{O}(\alpha^2)  \,.
L000887	\end{equation}
L000888	In the classical limit of Eq.~\eqref{eq:classicallimit}, one finds
L000889	\begin{equation}\label{eq:deltareg}
L000890	 \lim_{e^{S_0} \to \infty}  G_{\rm quantum} (\alpha , t) = \lim_{e^{S_0} \to \infty}  \left( G_{\rm delta}(\alpha) +G_{\rm sine}(\alpha, t) \right) =0 \,,
L000891	\end{equation}
L000892	which leads to a canonical choice for the subleading coefficient:
L000893	\begin{equation}
L000894	 \lim_{\talpha \to 0} \left( \partial_{\talpha} G^{\rm (reg)}_{\rm delta}(\alpha)  \right) =  -\frac{T_{\mt{H}}}{3} + 0 + \mathcal{O}\left( \left(\frac{1}{S_0}\right)^{n>0} \right)     \,,
L000895	\end{equation}
L000896	using the explicit expression of $G_{\rm sine}(\alpha, t)$ that was derived earlier in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Gsine}.
L000897	
L000898	Combining these results, we now compute the time evolution of the holographic or quantum complexity. Focusing on the regime $t < T_{\mt{H}}$, the generating function has been derived as
L000899	\begin{equation}
L000900	\begin{split}
L000901	\langle \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }} \rangle  &=G_{\rm classical}(\alpha, t) + G_{\rm delta}(\alpha) +G_{\rm sine}(\alpha, t) \\
L000902	&\approx e^{- t \tilde{\alpha} }  +  \frac{2}{\talpha T_{\mt{H}}} + \left(  -\dfrac{2 \talpha (\THH - t)-2 e^{-\talpha t}+e^{\talpha  (t-\THH)}+e^{-\talpha  (t+\THH)}}{\talpha ^2  T_{\mt{H}}^2} \right)  \,. \\
L000903	\end{split}
L000904	\end{equation}
L000905	After the regularization, we instead have
L000906	\begin{equation}
L000907	\begin{split}
L000908	\langle \widehat{e^{-\alpha \mC }}\rangle_{\text{reg}} &\overset{\alpha \to 0}{\approx}
L000909	  e^{- t \tilde{\alpha} } + \left( 1 - \talpha \frac{T_{\mt{H}}}{3}  + \mathcal{O}(\talpha^2) \right)- \left( e^{- t \tilde{\alpha} } + \talpha \frac{(t- T_{\mt{H}})^3}{3 T_{\mt{H}}^2}  + \mathcal{O}(\talpha^2)\right) \,,\\
L000910	&\approx 1  - \talpha \left( t - \frac{t^2}{T_{\mt{H}}} + \frac{t^3}{3 T_{\mt{H}}^2}\right)  +  \mathcal{O}(\talpha^2)  \,.
L000911	\end{split}
L000912	\end{equation}
L000913	Here, the subleading term defines the complexity growth, with $\tilde{\alpha}\approx M \alpha$ as before. More strictly speaking, the regularization is performed for getting the proper $\alpha \to 0$ limit.
L000914	Performing the derivative as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:defineC}, the time evolution of the complexity is thus derived as
L000915	\begin{tcolorbox}[top=-3pt, bottom=2pt]
L000916	\begin{equation}\label{eq:Cone}
L000917	\begin{split}
L000918	\langle \hat{\mC} \rangle  &= \lim_{\alpha \to 0} \left( -\partial_\alpha  \left( G_{\rm classical}(\alpha, t) + G^{(\rm reg)}_{\rm quantum} (\alpha, t) \right)  \right) \\
L000919	&\approx \text{Con} +
L000920	\begin{cases}
L000921	  M  \,
L000922	t\left(1-\frac{t}{T_{\mt{H}}}+\frac{t^2}{3 T_{\mt{H}}^2}\right)\,,\,\, (t < T_{\mt{H}})  \\[1em]
L000923	   \frac{1}{3} M \,T_{\mt{H}} \,, \quad\qquad\
L000924	   \,\, (t \ge T_{\mt{H}})
L000925	 \end{cases}\,.
L000926	\end{split}
L000927	\end{equation}
L000928	\end{tcolorbox}
L000929	This result gives the expression presented in the main text. Note that the overall additive constant remains undetermined. It originates from both the regularization of $G^{\rm (reg)}_{\rm delta}(\alpha)$ and the subleading term in the normalization factor of the pole discussed in Eq.~\eqref{eq:polenorm}. Nevertheless, the main feature is clear: the expectation value of quantum complexity measures exhibits a long-time linear growth that saturates to a plateau after the Heisenberg time $T{\mt{H}}$. A similar behavior was obtained for JT gravity in \cite{geodesic} by integration from the finite time derivative without explicitly implementing the regularization.
L000930	
L000931	Finally, it is important to emphasize that a plateau in the generating function does not automatically imply a similar plateau in the quantum complexity itself. For instance, an expression of the form $e^{-\alpha t} + \text{const}$ still results in a linear growth at late times. The plateau in quantum complexity arises only from a precise cancellation between the linear growth from the classical term and the linear decay from the quantum corrections. This cancellation is governed by the following condition
L000932	\begin{equation}
L000933	\text{Residue at $E_{ij}= - i \tilde{\alpha}$}  \quad \longrightarrow \quad   \lim_{\tilde{\alpha}  \to 0}  \left( \bar{\mD}^2   - \dfrac{\sinh^ 2(\pi e^{S_0} \bar{\mD} \tilde{\alpha} )}{(e^{S_0}\pi\tilde{\alpha} )^2}  \right) \times  t \, e^{-t \tilde{\alpha}}=0 \,.
L000934	\end{equation}
L000935	This is equivalent to the property of the original sine kernel with zero energy separation, \ie
L000936	\begin{equation}
L000937	  \lim_{E_{ij} \to 0}  \left(  \bar{D}(E_i) \bar{D}(E_j)
L000938	     -\frac{\sin^2(\pi e^{S_0} \bar{D}(\bar{E})E_{ij})}{(e^{S_0}\pi E_{ij})^2}  \right) =0  \,,
L000939	\end{equation}
L000940	which is precisely the manifestation of level repulsion. In a separate appendix, we generalize this conclusion to more generic quantum systems that exhibit quantum chaos.
